        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "5509c60e-8ba3-3640-938a-4062eb476743",
                "word": "Jemima",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d9700c10-0839-3dc8-94f3-67b89fc0d72b",
                        "entry_id": "5509c60e-8ba3-3640-938a-4062eb476743",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female given name from Hebrew."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "dd8c5a0b-ca35-440d-81f4-5c7fd4e1f7a3",
                                "sense_id": "d9700c10-0839-3dc8-94f3-67b89fc0d72b",
                                "text": "He had also seven sons and three daughters. And he called the name of the first, Jemima; and the name of the second, Kezia; and the name of the third, Kerenhappuch. And in all the land were no women found so fair as the daughters of Job: and their father gave them inheritance among their brethren."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "46dd20a6-8b73-47f6-aa25-c6f221bf5dda",
                                "sense_id": "d9700c10-0839-3dc8-94f3-67b89fc0d72b",
                                "text": "-- and at last Jemima was fixed upon, because it would do either for a Scripture name or for a heroine out of a book.\"\n\"I did not know Jemima was a Scripture name,\" said Ruth.\n\"Oh yes, it is. One of Job's daughters; Jemima, Kezia, and Keren-Happuch. There are a good many Jemimas in the world, and some Kezias, but I never heard of a Keren-Happuch; and yet we know just as much of one as of another. People really like a pretty name, whether Scripture or out of it.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0835bf1-4eab-40c2-8f21-969c4e4f32b6",
                                "sense_id": "d9700c10-0839-3dc8-94f3-67b89fc0d72b",
                                "text": "But then, thought Kiki, they were brought up that way, these white American boys: I'm the Aunt Jemima on the cookie boxes of their childhoods, the pair of thick ankles Tom and Jerry played around. Of course they find me funny."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ae9770fe-165c-40dd-b0a7-ba0c5730cb8f",
                                "sense_id": "d9700c10-0839-3dc8-94f3-67b89fc0d72b",
                                "text": "Fancy southern names which she couldn't remember five minutes after they'd told her. Camilla? Amelia? Jemima? It didn't matter. Ashworth would have made a note of them."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
